v1.5
    Fix: the USB controller rebind no longer runs blind. It re-enumerates
        every device on the modem's controller, and v1.4 was released on the
        claim that nothing else useful sat there. That claim was wrong: on
        the reference install the other device is the VE.Direct cable of the
        BMV-712, the active battery service, with DVCC on. The rung now lists
        the other devices on the controller and refuses when there are any,
        logging what it would have taken down; the ladder then moves on. The
        check runs in the service before it spends a destructive slot, and
        again in the helper, so no caller can bypass it. HCI_REBIND_SHARED=1
        accepts the collateral explicitly.
    Fix: the link watchdog remembers the modem's controller while the modem
        is visible, so its last step still knows what to target once the
        modem has left the bus, and it recognises a refusal instead of
        counting it as a destructive action.
    Fix: a regular-expression back-reference and two escape sequences in the
        shell helpers had been turned into raw control characters by the
        editing tool; one of them would have been deleted by setup's own CR
        stripping, silently disabling the Windows line-ending tolerance of the
        helper's config reader.
    Fix: exactly one link watchdog. Three copies were found running side
        by side on the Pi, one still on v1.4 code: the TERM trap removed the
        pidfile and then let the script carry on, so every kill left an
        unlocked watchdog behind and the next start added another. A signal
        now stops it, only the owner of the lock may remove it, and a process
        scan backs the lock up. The watchdog honours LINKWATCH=0 itself.
    Fix: a watchdog is recognised by its exact command shape
        ('<sh> <path>/e3372_linkwatch.sh'), not by its name: a looser match
        killed the very SSH shell that was running the stopper.
    Fix: setup installs every helper by rename instead of overwriting it in
        place (a running script was being rewritten under its feet), stops
        every running watchdog and starts exactly one on the new code.
    Fix: exactly one DHCP client on wwan0. A udhcpc was found alive after
        21 hours: two had been started in the same second, the second one
        overwrote the first one's pid, and every restart since had killed
        only the pidfile's client while the orphan fought the current one
        over the lease. Clients are now found by command line; a restart
        stops all of them, a minute-by-minute check keeps only one, and the
        USB helper and the link bring-up script do the same.
    Fix: the USB helper and the link bring-up script had the same trap
        shape as the watchdog; a signal now undoes and stops.
    Validated in situ (2026-09-23): USB re-enumeration run three times -
        normally, with the helper killed between its two writes (repaired by
        its guard at 60 s), and with the helper and its guard killed
        (repaired by the watchdog from the intent journal at 100 s). The
        BMV-712 on the neighbouring port stayed present throughout. The
        controller rebind refused on the real sysfs, and acted only on
        explicit acceptance (tested against dummy bind/unbind files).
    Tests: 74 offline scenarios; the shell benches run the real helpers
        against a fake sysfs tree and real background processes, pass under
        BusyBox on the Pi, and lint every shell file for the editing
        artefacts (a backslash-newline turned into a literal '\n', raw
        control characters) that slipped through more than once.

v1.4
    Measured on the hardware on 2026-09-22, and it changes the design:
      - AT^RESET is the ONLY command that restarts this firmware, and the
        only one that escapes the state a radio-off leaves it in. Proven:
        with the radio off, AT+CFUN=1 and AT+COPS=0 both answer
        +CME ERROR: 100; AT^RESET answers OK, the device leaves the bus for
        2.7 s and comes back registered 10 s later.
      - AT+CFUN=1,1 restarts NOTHING. It answers OK and the device never
        leaves the bus. v1.3 used it as its "modem reset" rung.
      - No host-side USB action changes anything inside the modem. The port
        really loses power on paper, and the modem still comes back 0.5 s
        later with its firmware state intact. De-authorising, disabling the
        port and rebinding the controller are three strengths of the same
        bus reset, useful only when the driver is wedged.
    New ladder, every rung a single command (so the service can never be
        killed between two halves of a sequence): radio on if it is off,
        automatic operator selection, AT^RESET, USB re-enumeration,
        controller rebind. RECOVERY_LEVEL now means 0 observe, 1 safe AT,
        2 + modem reset, 3 + USB, 4 + controller.
    New: commands that can strand the modem are refused at a single choke
        point (AT+CFUN=0/4/6/7, AT+COPS=2, AT+CGATT=0). The package can no
        longer create a state it may be unable to leave - which is what the
        v1.3 radio cycle risked doing every time it ran.
    New: a destructive rung is only credited when the modem really
        re-enumerated (its USB devnum changed), recorded before the rung and
        persisted, since the rung itself usually kills this service. A rung
        that answered OK and changed nothing escalates immediately instead
        of burning its settle time.
    New: the USB helper writes its intent to /run before acting, guarded by
        a trap, a forked child and a repair pass run by the service at
        start-up and by the link watchdog every minute. A helper killed
        between its two writes can no longer leave the port down.
    New: the link watchdog talks to the modem FIRST (AT+CFUN? then
        AT^RESET) and only then touches USB, escalating to the port cycle
        and finally to the controller rebind, sharing a destructive budget
        with the service's ladder and never rebooting the GX.
    Fix: a silent AT poll no longer wipes the current reason and restarts
        the grace timer, so an intermittently mute modem now reaches the
        ladder.
    Fix: a ladder whose every rung is held by the reset budget is deferred,
        not counted, so it no longer doubles the backoff for a ladder that
        never ran a command.
    Fix: the traffic reason waits 30 min instead of 6, uses the interface's
        received-byte counter to overrule a failed ping (an APN that filters
        ICMP no longer costs the modem a restart), and its verdict is taken
        on the session the last re-dial created.
    Fix: AT timeouts are measured on the monotonic clock. This GX has no NTP
        and its wall clock is hours off, so a clock step could turn a 3 s
        timeout into an unbounded wait.
    Fix: the link watchdog no longer sources the user-editable config (a
        stray CR disarmed it, and a syntax error would have executed), and
        no longer rebinds every USB controller - only the modem's own.
    Fix: SystemShim.write() added for sysfs, which os.replace() cannot
        touch; every repair path depended on it.
    New: /Modem/Cfun on D-Bus, and the radio state is read at every poll -
        v1.3 had no way to see that the radio was off at all.
    New: e3372_at.py shipped with the package. A shell redirection to
        /dev/cdc-wdm0 silently does nothing, which cost a misleading test;
        the ReadMe recommended it.
    Tests: 67 offline scenarios plus a shell bench that runs the real
        helpers against a fake sysfs tree. The fake modem now models the
        measured truth: AT^RESET restarts, AT+CFUN=1,1 does not, a bus reset
        preserves the firmware state, and a radio-off refuses every write.

v1.3
    New: last-resort link watchdog (/data/e3372_linkwatch.sh), started
        detached by the service so that it survives the service being
        killed. Every rung of the ladder needs the modem's tty or
        /dev/cdc-wdm0; both vanish when the modem leaves the USB bus, and
        serial-starter then kills the service - so nothing was left to
        reset the USB port, the one action that brings such a modem back.
        The watchdog does only that, once wwan0 has had no address and no
        default route for LINKWATCH_DOWN_SECONDS (15 min by default), at
        most once every 30 min, and never while the session is alive.
        LINKWATCH=0 disables it; LINKWATCH_HCI_RESET=1 additionally allows
        rebinding the USB controller when the modem is not on the bus at
        all (opt-in: it re-enumerates the other devices of that
        controller).
    New: recovery ladder for every state in which the modem cannot be
        dialled. The watchdog used to give up silently whenever the modem
        was not registered (CREG 0/2/3/4): a "Registration denied" from the
        network left the GX offline for 11 days, until a reboot. It now
        escalates, after a grace period, through a network re-selection
        (AT+COPS=2/0), a radio cycle (AT+CFUN=0/1), a modem reset
        (AT+CFUN=1,1) and a USB port reset, with a growing pause between
        full ladders (15 min doubling up to 4 h) and never more than two
        destructive resets per 30 min. The same ladder covers a mute AT
        port, a missing or failed SIM, a data session that will not dial,
        and a session that passes no traffic. The GX itself is never
        rebooted: every action is confined to the modem.
    New: RECOVERY_LEVEL (0-4, 0 = observe only) and RECOVERY_TIMESCALE in
        /data/e3372-config.conf. Heavy commands go through /dev/cdc-wdm0 so
        that a slow answer never blocks the service.
    New: SIM PIN sent from /Settings/Modem/PIN, once; a wrong PIN clears the
        setting (three wrong attempts would lock the SIM). /SimStatus now
        uses the codes the GUI displays (no SIM, PIN, PUK, failure, ...).
    New: recovery state persisted in /run (survives a service restart, not a
        reboot), persistent journal in /data/log/e3372/recovery.log,
        /Recovery/* and /Signal/* paths on D-Bus, signal quality
        (RSSI/RSRP/SINR/RSRQ) logged every minute and on every transition.
    Fix: the dial no longer blocks the service for up to 22 s. The previous
        session is confirmed down before the new one is requested, and the
        re-dial backoff is only reset after 60 s of stable session: 40% of
        the drops used to be a fresh session dying seconds after the dial,
        re-dialled every 30 s.
    Fix: a service restart keeps a session that is already up instead of
        re-dialling it (and cutting the remote access with it).
    Fix: a stale DHCP client no longer blocks the address forever.
    Fix: the connectivity probe accepts several hosts and is suspended for
        6 h instead of being disabled for good.
    Fix: unsolicited modem reports (^RSSI, ^HCSQ, ^MODE, ...) can no longer
        be mistaken for a response; AT^CURC=0 at start.
    Fix: status polling stops at the first timeout, so a mute modem costs
        one timeout per poll instead of six.

v1.2
    Fix: /PPPStatus now follows the Victron PPP_STATUS enum (0 down,
        1 connecting, 2 up). The session-up state was published as 1,
        which the GUI renders as "Data link (PPP) status: connecting"
        even with the connection established. 1 is now published only
        while actually dialling.
    Fix: modem identification (/Model, /IMEI) is retried from the periodic
        update until it succeeds. A single AT timeout at startup used to
        leave both empty until the service was restarted.
    Cleanup: USB_PID removed from e3372-config.conf - nothing ever read it
        (the USB PIDs are hardcoded in the udev rules).
    ReadMe: repository URL corrected (flmusic -> florent1080).

v1.1
    Fix: the data session is now watched and re-dialled automatically.
        The session could drop (operator side) while wwan0 kept its stale
        address and default route. Nothing detected it and nothing re-dialled,
        so the GX lost internet until the next reboot.
    Fix: /Connected no longer forced to 1 by a leftover address on wwan0.
        AT^NDISSTATQRY is now the only source of truth for the data session.
        The UI used to report "Internet: Online" with the session closed.
    Fix: /PPPStatus is now actually published. It was declared once at
        startup and never written, so the UI showed "Data link (PPP) status:
        Offline" permanently, including when the connection worked.
    Fix: e3372_connect.sh no longer opens a serial port. It hardcoded
        /dev/ttyUSB0, which on a Venus OS install is normally a VE.Direct
        adapter and not the modem: the AT commands never reached the modem,
        and the port was switched to 115200 baud under the VE.Direct service.
        Dialling now belongs solely to dbus-modem-e3372.py, which
        serial-starter binds to the modem's real tty.
    Fix: udhcpc no longer runs with -q, so the DHCP client stays resident,
        renews the lease and restores the address and default route.
    Fix: e3372_connect.sh takes a lock; it used to race up to three
        concurrent udhcpc against each other on a single hotplug.
    Fix: the stale address is flushed before re-dialling, so the old default
        route can no longer black-hole traffic.
    Fix: /Settings/Modem/Connect is honoured (connect / hang up on change).
    Fix: the serial port is reopened after an I/O error instead of leaving
        the service permanently deaf.
    New: PROBE_HOST in /data/e3372-config.conf - host pinged periodically to
        detect a session that is up but no longer passing traffic. Set it
        empty to disable when the APN filters ICMP.

v1.0
    initial release
    Huawei E3372h (NCM mode, USB PID 12d1:1506) support for Venus OS
    D-Bus modem service (com.victronenergy.modem) with signal, network, IMEI
    NCM data connection via AT^NDISDUP + DHCP on wwan0
    udev rules for serial-starter and hotplug
    configurable APN via /data/e3372-config.conf
